#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2]$." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad y = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad z = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi - xj + zk \\\\\n&= \\cos (πt)i - \\sin (πt)j + (2t - t^{2})k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin (πt))i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos (πt))j + \\frac{d}{dt}(2t - t^{2})k \\\\\n&= π\\cos (πt)i - π\\sin (πt)j + 2(1 - t)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (\\cos (πt)i - \\sin (πt)j + (2t - t^{2})k) \\cdot (π\\cos (πt)i - π\\sin (πt)j + 2(1 - t)k) \\\\\n&= \\cos (πt) \\cdot π\\cos (πt) + (-\\sin (πt)) \\cdot (- π\\sin (πt)) + (2t - t^{2}) \\cdot 2(1 - t) \\\\\n&= π\\cos^{2} (πt) + π\\sin^{2} (πt) + 2(2t - t^{2})(1 - t) \\\\\n&= π(\\cos^{2} (πt) + \\sin^{2} (πt)) + 2(2t - 3t^{2} + t^{3}) \\\\\n&= π + 4t - 6t^{2} + 2t^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ = ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{2}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}π + 4t - 6t^{2} + 2t^{3}dt \\\\\n&= [πt + 2t^{2} - 2t^{3} + \\frac{1}{2}t^{4}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= [2π + 8 - 16 + 8] - [0] \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}$,\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle$, and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S$, then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $z=1$. One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation\n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) = 0 \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncurlF \\mid _{z = g(x, y)} &= ⟨ - xz,-yz, z^{2} + x⟩ \\mid _{z = 1} \\\\\n&= ⟨- x_(⏟)_(P),-y_(⏟)_(Q), 1 + x_(⏟)_(R)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS &= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(- (-x) \\cdot 0 - (-y) \\cdot 0 + 1 + x)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(1 + x)dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nof radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(1 + x)dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2π}(1 + r\\cos θ) \\cdot rdθdr \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{2π}r + r^{2}\\cos θdθdr \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[rθ + r^{2}\\sin θ]_{0}^{2π}dr \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2πrdr \\\\\n&= π[r^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $[MATH: t \\in [0,1).]$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k}$, evaluate the integral\n$\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}$." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S$, then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad y = t^2-t, \\qquad z = t^2-t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= i + xj + yk \\\\\n&= i + (t^{2} - t^{3})j + (t^{2} - t)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t^{3})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - t)k \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t^{2})i + (2t - 1)j + (2t - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (i + (t^{2} - t^{3})j + (t^{2} - t)k) \\cdot ((2t - 3t^{2})i + (2t - 1)j + (2t - 1)k) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (2t - 3t^{2}) + (t^{2} - t^{3}) \\cdot (2t - 1) + (t^{2} - t) \\cdot (2t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t - 3t^{2} + (-t^{3} + 2t^{2} - t) \\cdot (2t - 1) \\\\\n&= 2t - 3t^{2} - 2t^{4} + t^{3} + 4t^{3} - 2t^{2} - 2t^{2} + t \\\\\n&= 3t - 7t^{2} + 5t^{3} - 2t^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)curlF \\cdot dS &= ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(3t - 7t^{2} + 5t^{3} - 2t^{4})dt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{3}{2}t^{2} - \\frac{7}{3}t^{3} + \\frac{5}{4}t^{4} - \\frac{2}{5}t^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{7}{3} + \\frac{5}{4} - \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{60}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2016fc5-d910-5d89-94c7-22086ba208ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130870} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b13363-d1b8-5235-ae4b-62a35c52b74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130871} {:answer/id #uui